spec:
  grid: 10-11 rows x 10-11 cols, square [observed]
  background: 7 [observed]; most-frequent color
  rectangles:
    count: 2-4 filled rectangles, each with a distinct non-bg, non-teal color
    size: 2-5 rows x 2-5 cols each
    teal_corner: exactly one corner cell per rect replaced by teal (8); any of the 4 corners
    body: all cells except the teal corner are the rect's color
  placement:
    shifted rects (output positions) must not overlap each other
    shifted rect must stay in-bounds; rect may touch grid edge only on sides opposite the shift direction
    min gap >= 1 cell between any two output rects (Chebyshev)
    occupancy ~30-36% of grid is non-bg
  transform:
    shift each rect 1 cell diagonally toward its teal corner (teal at top-left -> shift (-1,-1), etc.)
    output replaces teal pixel with rect color; no teal in output

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size 10-11 square, bg=7, choose 2-4 distinct colors (not 7 or 8).
    2. For each rect pick size (2-5 x 2-5) and assign a random corner for the teal pixel; this determines shift direction (dr,dc) as the negation of the corner offset.
    3. Place the COMPLETED (no teal) rects on the grid as the output, ensuring no overlaps and min-gap >= 1 between any pair, and each rect is fully in-bounds.
    4. Derive input: shift each rect by (-dr,-dc) (i.e. away from the teal corner by 1 cell); verify the shifted rect stays in-bounds. Replace the designated corner cell with teal (8). Erase output footprints that are not covered by any input rect; fill with bg.
```
